rocessed the state after last step? \n    # The loop increments steps after updating state. So if we exit because steps >= max_steps, we haven't checked the new state. \n    # So after loop, check current state for final:\n    if state in trans and trans[state]['kind'] == 'final':\n        return trans[state]['value']\n    else:\n        return None\n\nBut careful: The loop condition is steps < max_steps. At start, steps=0. Each iteration we process state, then update state, then increment steps. After steps becomes max_steps, loop ends. So we have processed exactly max_steps transitions (actions). So the resulting state is after the last action. We should check if that state is final. So the above logic works.\n\nEdge cases: default_next always present. Assume all keys exist.\n\nWe need to escape the Python source string for JSON. We'll write it as a string with newlines escaped as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". But we can also use triple quotes in the JSON string? No, JSON string must be enclosed in double quotes and internal quotes escaped. Better to write the Python code as a single line or use \\n. I'll write it as a compact single-line code for simplicity, but it's fine to have newlines escaped.\n\nI'll generate the JSON: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    p = case.get('payload', case)\\n    state = p['initial_state']\\n    max_steps = p['max_steps']\\n    obs = p['tool_observations']\\n    trans = p['transitions']\\n    steps = 0\\n    while steps < max_steps:\\n        t = trans[state]\\n        if t['kind'] == 'final':\\n            return t['value']\\n        action = t['action']\\n        observation = obs[action]\\n        next_map = t['next_by_observation']\\n        if observation in next_map:\\n            state = next_map[observation]\\n        else:\\n            state = t['default_next']\\n        steps += 1\\n    if state in trans and trans[state]['kind'] == 'final':\\n        return trans[state]['value']\\n    return None\"}